{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794862132,"nonce":"ZCw9L/QvF8+YT/dC0hhN1pMEpHM=","title":"Lock.wait() in a Haxe thread on wasm returns false at once when the thread cannot suspend","message":"Found while chasing f6caebf, 2026-11-16, in headless Chrome with a Haxe program (thread asks the main\nevent loop to run a callback that releases a Lock, then `handled.wait()` and prints the result).\n- Native, any mode: the wait blocks until the release and returns true.\n- wasm32-wasip1-threads, 0.1.20 and the local build: it returns false after 0 to 1 ms, whether the thread\n  runs on an agent or on the main scheduler.\n\nFrom the source, not confirmed by tracing: a thread body is a fiber, `Lock.wait` parks it, and a wasm\nfiber cannot suspend without the fiber transform, so the park returns at once as not notified. The\ntimed and untimed waits then both decline. Callers that ignore the result (hxn's delivery thread) go\nround again; callers that use it as \"released\" are wrong.\n\nTo decide: on wasm without the transform, a thread on an agent could wait on the OS (as a thread the\nruntime did not create does), and a thread on the main scheduler cannot block at all, which is a limit\nto name in the docs.","files":null}]}